WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.400
If you think rocket science sounds difficult.&nbsp;
Just wait until I tell you about this company,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:10.320
SpaceRyde. Instead of just launching rockets&nbsp;
from the ground, they use balloons to carry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:15.920
rockets into the upper atmosphere before launching&nbsp;
them. And surprisingly, it actually works! At the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:21.200
center of the project is this guy: “My name is&nbsp;
Sohrab Haghighat and I’m the co-founder and CEO&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:25.920
of SpaceRyde” He’s a super talented engineer based&nbsp;
in Canada and he’s been building this company for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:31.440
the past five years. Recently, we’ve seen a bit of&nbsp;
an arms race to build bigger and bigger rockets.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:37.040
Starship usually gets all the attention, and for&nbsp;
good reason. It can deliver a payload of a hundred&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:42.400
metric tons to low earth orbit. SpaceRyde on&nbsp;
the other hand, can only deliver about a hundred&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:48.000
kilos. That’s a thousand times less. But there’s&nbsp;
actually a very good reason for this approach.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:55.840
“What I want to do is enable small satellites&nbsp;
and small payloads that are going to space&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.840 --> 00:01:00.320
so they can achieve their mission” 1:34 See, the&nbsp;
space industry is growing so quickly now that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:04.720
there are needs at both ends of the payload&nbsp;
spectrum. Starship and bigger rockets will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:08.960
obviously be incredibly important for huge&nbsp;
payloads, but the high price can lock out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:13.760
smaller projects. Ridesharing on SpaceX rockets&nbsp;
will work for some companies, but you’ll still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:18.560
need to deal with narrow launch schedules. If&nbsp;
SpaceX is building a massive eighteen wheeler,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:24.640
SpaceRyde is building a taxi service. It’s highly&nbsp;
customizable and incredibly affordable. So let’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:30.320
dig into how this all works. SpaceRyde combines&nbsp;
three main technologies to deliver payloads to low&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:35.840
earth orbit. The first is the balloon and this&nbsp;
is by far the easiest part. But before we talk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:40.640
about how the balloon works, we need to answer the&nbsp;
obvious question of why you would even want to use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:45.200
a balloon in the first place. Well, even though&nbsp;
we don’t notice it when we’re just walking around.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:50.320
The atmosphere is actually pretty thick down here&nbsp;
on earth. All the air creates an incredible amount&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:55.040
of drag on a rocket while it’s taking off. If&nbsp;
you want to counteract this drag, you just need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.040 --> 00:02:00.160
more power, which means a bigger rocket. That,&nbsp;
of course, means even more drag. This isn’t a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.160 --> 00:02:04.880
problem for big rockets with big payloads, but&nbsp;
if you're trying to deliver smaller payloads,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:09.120
you’ll wind up wasting lots of energy just&nbsp;
plowing through the first layer of Earth’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:13.520
atmosphere. “You need a cheat and the balloon is&nbsp;
our cheat, since it gives you a free ride to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:18.240
edge of the atmosphere” - 2:55 There’s another&nbsp;
benefit though. During every rocket launch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:34.720
at some point you’ll hear the ground crew&nbsp;
say “we’ve reached MaxQ”. What that means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:38.400
is that the rocket has just reached the&nbsp;
air pressure limits due to the atmosphere,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:44.000
and it needs to throttle back. When you launch a&nbsp;
balloon 30 kilometers above the ground, MaxQ isn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:49.440
an issue, so you can design a completely different&nbsp;
rocket. Sohrab and his team have done just that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:54.640
but before we dive into their rocket design, let’s&nbsp;
talk about the history of space balloons. Now, I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.640 --> 00:02:59.040
don’t know anyone who would watch a balloon launch&nbsp;
over a rocket launch, but they’re actually an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:04.400
incredibly important part of modern science. NASA&nbsp;
alone launches a new mission every month through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:09.120
their Scientific Balloon Program. And balloons&nbsp;
are so simple to make that even high-school&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:14.320
students can launch stuff into space using them.&nbsp;
Balloons are incredibly reliable at this point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:20.320
but more importantly, they are incredibly cheap.&nbsp;
“So the balloon as far as we care about it is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.320 --> 00:03:25.680
commodity. So we buy our balloon.” The balloons&nbsp;
they use are made out of simple plastic materials,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:30.000
not unlike a shopping bag. And they fill it&nbsp;
with hydrogen gas, which provides plenty of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:34.480
lift capacity and is extremely affordable.&nbsp;
If you’re ever looking at prices of anything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:39.440
in the space industry, you’ll be shocked to find&nbsp;
anything selling for less than a million dollars.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.440 --> 00:03:44.480
Even small parts on big rockets can be extremely&nbsp;
expensive, and those costs get passed through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:49.840
directly to launch costs. This is why, even though&nbsp;
the cost of delivery payloads to space is falling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.840 --> 00:03:55.600
significantly on a per kilo basis, individual&nbsp;
launches are still millions of dollars. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.600 --> 00:04:00.240
the cheapest rocket on a per-launch basis right&nbsp;
now is provided by Astra, and it’s still around $3&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:06.000
million dollars. But SpaceRyde will offer launches&nbsp;
for one third that cost and has plans to quickly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:10.720
push the price below a million dollars per launch.&nbsp;
This could be really disrupting at scale and it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.720 --> 00:04:15.600
incredibly impressive for such a small company.&nbsp;
After all, Astra has raised hundreds of millions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:20.720
of dollars and is a public company. SpaceRyde&nbsp;
has raised less than $5 million dollars so far.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:26.320
Leveraging existing and reliable technology is key&nbsp;
to so many startup success stories, but the only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.320 --> 00:04:30.800
reason balloons are so widely accepted in the&nbsp;
aerospace industry is because they’ve been in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:37.200
use for over a hundred years now. All the way back&nbsp;
in 1912, an Austrian physicist named Victor Hess&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:41.840
used a hot air balloon to measure radiation levels&nbsp;
in the atmosphere. At the time, people believed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.840 --> 00:04:47.760
that radiation came from the earth, so the higher&nbsp;
you went, the lower the radiation would be, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:53.120
his measurements showed that the radiation levels&nbsp;
actually increased at high altitudes, suggesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.120 --> 00:04:58.800
that the radiation was coming from space. He had&nbsp;
essentially discovered cosmic rays and he won the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:05.200
Nobel Prize in Physics for his work. Flash forward&nbsp;
to 1949, and balloons again played a critical role&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.200 --> 00:05:10.400
in atmospheric research. A group of American&nbsp;
naval scientists attached a rocket to a balloon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.400 --> 00:05:15.600
and launched it off of the U.S.S Norton Sound.&nbsp;
They called this rocket-balloon a “rockoon” and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:20.400
even though it did successfully launch the rocket,&nbsp;
the fact that they couldn’t steer the contraption&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:25.360
really limited its usefulness. But SpaceRyde has&nbsp;
solved this problem with their second key piece&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.360 --> 00:05:32.560
of technology: the rocket carrier. “Rocket Carrier&nbsp;
is the key ingredient of this business” No one is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.560 --> 00:05:37.280
going to pay SpaceRyde to launch their satellite&nbsp;
into orbit if they can’t guarantee where it will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.280 --> 00:05:42.080
wind up orbiting. A big part of the SpaceRyde&nbsp;
value proposition is that small satellite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.080 --> 00:05:47.040
companies can not only choose when to launch, but&nbsp;
they can decide the exact orbit that they want for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:52.240
their satellite. That type of customizability is&nbsp;
largely unattainable via larger launch companies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.240 --> 00:05:56.400
unless of course you’re willing to fork over the&nbsp;
millions of dollars it costs to cover the entire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.400 --> 00:06:01.600
launch. The SpaceRyde rocket carrier attaches&nbsp;
the rocket to the balloon and, more importantly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.600 --> 00:06:05.600
makes sure that the rocket is pointing in the&nbsp;
right direction when it launches. The carrier&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.600 --> 00:06:10.160
itself looks kind of like a quadrotor drone,&nbsp;
but there are some really important calculations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.160 --> 00:06:14.880
going on to make subtle corrections and make&nbsp;
sure the rocket is aligned properly at launch.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.520 --> 00:06:20.720
Air launches have been tried before, but they’ve&nbsp;
never really made much economic sense. The most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.720 --> 00:06:26.000
well-known air-launched rocket is the Northrop&nbsp;
Grumman Pegasus, which has been in operation since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:32.080
1990. Even though Pegasus has had a solid track&nbsp;
record over the last thirty years. It still costs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.080 --> 00:06:38.080
$40 million dollars per launch and has a host of&nbsp;
other problems. Back in 2012, Elon Musk actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.080 --> 00:06:41.600
addressed the Pegasus vehicle directly: “When&nbsp;
you drop the rocket, you have a slight problem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.600 --> 00:06:45.360
because you're not going in the right direction.&nbsp;
If you see what Orbital Sciences did with Pegasus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:48.960
they had the delta wing to do the turning&nbsp;
maneuver. And then you added this wing which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.960 --> 00:06:52.880
is added mass. And the net is that it’s not&nbsp;
great. They started out with the Pegasus air&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.880 --> 00:06:57.280
launch vehicle and subsequently they haven’t done&nbsp;
any air launch vehicles.” Simply put: That delta&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.280 --> 00:07:02.080
wing on top of the Pegasus rocket is inefficient.&nbsp;
But SpaceRyde doesn’t need one of those on their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.080 --> 00:07:06.880
rocket because the rocket carrier can position&nbsp;
the rocket automatically before it launches.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.880 --> 00:07:12.000
It’s an interesting situation, where advances&nbsp;
in software lead to simpler hardware solutions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:17.280
Similar to how self-driving cars have been able to&nbsp;
move away from complicated lidar systems and just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.280 --> 00:07:22.320
use normal cameras because of advances in machine&nbsp;
learning. This is always a great place to be in as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.320 --> 00:07:27.280
a startup. Software is getting better at a much&nbsp;
faster rate than hardware, so anytime you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.280 --> 00:07:31.920
leverage the cutting edge of software development,&nbsp;
you’ll have an advantage. And Sohrab knows this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.920 --> 00:07:37.280
intimately. Before he founded SpaceRyde, he&nbsp;
actually worked on self-driving cars at Cruise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.280 --> 00:07:42.240
Automation. While there, he worked on Planning&nbsp;
and Controls, which has a remarkable amount of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.240 --> 00:07:48.240
overlap with the work he’s doing now at SpaceRyde.&nbsp;
See every autonomous vehicle has to do two things.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.240 --> 00:07:53.200
First, it had to perceive the world, which&nbsp;
basically means stitching together all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.200 --> 00:07:59.360
camera and sensor data into a 3d representation.&nbsp;
Kind of like a video game. Then, the system has to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.360 --> 00:08:03.600
decide what to do with that data, where to&nbsp;
actually drive the car safely, which means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.600 --> 00:08:08.960
planning out a safe path and then sending inputs&nbsp;
to the steering wheel and gas pedal accordingly.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.960 --> 00:08:13.840
Sohrab worked on that second part at Cruise, and&nbsp;
it’s a perfect fit for SpaceRyde. The perception&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.840 --> 00:08:18.000
problem is a lot simpler in a balloon, since&nbsp;
there are no pedestrians to deal with up in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:26.000
the sky. But controlling the rocket carrier is&nbsp;
remarkably similar to controlling a car. When&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.000 --> 00:08:30.720
the team goes to launch a balloon, they know the&nbsp;
general trajectory and can plan out a basic course&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.720 --> 00:08:35.760
of action. But once everything lifts off, the&nbsp;
autonomous systems have to take over. Being able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.760 --> 00:08:40.240
to reliably steer the carrier without a pilot&nbsp;
is really tricky, but doing this with software&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.240 --> 00:08:44.480
means it’s ultimately more reliable and more&nbsp;
affordable. So once the balloon is up there, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.480 --> 00:08:48.960
the rocket carrier is in the right position. What&nbsp;
happens? Well, they have to launch the rocket.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.960 --> 00:08:53.040
But SpaceRyde rockets are custom made to take&nbsp;
advantage of the high altitude. “When you look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.040 --> 00:08:58.480
at rockets they are often tall and sender. There&nbsp;
is a reason. Because you are minimizing drag.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.480 --> 00:09:04.320
There is no drag concern when you are launching&nbsp;
at 30 kilometer altitude. So your rocket can be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.320 --> 00:09:10.320
short and chubby. And a fat rocket is actually&nbsp;
a good rocket.” This “fat” rocket design is more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.320 --> 00:09:14.800
than just funny though. It actually serves&nbsp;
a very specific purpose. Although the design&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.800 --> 00:09:20.720
is unorthodox, these SpaceRyde “fat” rockets find&nbsp;
the perfect balance between structural integrity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.720 --> 00:09:26.240
fuel efficiency, and payload capacity. This is&nbsp;
super critical, because SpaceRyde is all about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.240 --> 00:09:31.040
unlocking the small payload delivery market, which&nbsp;
is actually growing incredibly quickly. Pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.040 --> 00:09:36.480
much everyone knows about StarLink at this point,&nbsp;
but global internet is far from the only useful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.480 --> 00:09:41.680
application for small satellites. Increasingly,&nbsp;
satellites are getting smaller and smaller, all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.680 --> 00:09:46.640
while being more versatile and useful. This can be&nbsp;
hard to visualize, because when most of us think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.640 --> 00:09:51.280
about satellites we imagine the hubble telescope&nbsp;
or the international space station. You know, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.280 --> 00:09:56.720
type of space stuff that’s big enough to point a&nbsp;
camera at. But satellites are getting small, like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.720 --> 00:10:02.640
really small. Traditionally, a small satellite is&nbsp;
defined as anything under one thousand kilograms.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.640 --> 00:10:07.920
But SpaceRyde is only planning on carrying between&nbsp;
80 and 150 kilograms. That’s not a problem though&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.920 --> 00:10:13.440
because micro satellites or “cubesats” typically&nbsp;
weigh less than 100 kilograms, with some as light&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.440 --> 00:10:19.600
as just 10 kilos. There are also “nanosatellites”&nbsp;
now that weigh under 10 kilos, “picosatellites”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.600 --> 00:10:24.480
that weigh less than one kilo, and people are even&nbsp;
prototyping “femto-satellites” that weigh less&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.480 --> 00:10:31.120
than 100 grams. At that point, it’s basically&nbsp;
just a simple chip floating around up there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.120 --> 00:10:36.240
but as we’ve seen with smartphones, you can fit a&nbsp;
lot on a single chip these days. So, we have a few&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.240 --> 00:10:41.600
technology trends all accelerating and converging&nbsp;
at the same time to make this a reality. Demand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.600 --> 00:10:46.080
for satellites is increasing exponentially,&nbsp;
satellites are getting smaller and smaller,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.080 --> 00:10:50.560
and autonomous control software is getting&nbsp;
better. All of these trends point to an easy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.560 --> 00:10:56.480
win for SpaceRyde right? Well, not so fast. Sohrab&nbsp;
has been working on this for the past five years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.480 --> 00:11:01.040
and I could tell from my conversation with him&nbsp;
that it hasn’t all been easy. First off, he’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.040 --> 00:11:06.320
doing all of this in Canada, which hasn’t had an&nbsp;
active launch program in the country for decades.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.320 --> 00:11:10.720
Canada does have astronauts, but they partner&nbsp;
with the United States and European Space Agency&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.720 --> 00:11:15.200
for actual launch capabilities. Fortunately,&nbsp;
Sohrab has an amazing partner in his wife and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.200 --> 00:11:20.720
co-founder Saharnaz. She has an MBA and serves&nbsp;
as SpaceRyde’s chief operating officer, so when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.720 --> 00:11:25.680
it was time to work with the Canadian government&nbsp;
to get regulatory approval, she stepped up and got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.680 --> 00:11:31.360
it done in record time. Often the best founding&nbsp;
teams have different but complementary skill sets.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.360 --> 00:11:35.920
After all, it doesn’t matter how well designed&nbsp;
your rocket is, if no one will let you launch it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.920 --> 00:11:41.200
But there’s one more key takeaway that I love from&nbsp;
this story, and it’s about determination. Sohrab&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.200 --> 00:11:45.200
probably could have coasted as an engineer in&nbsp;
Silicon Valley for his entire career. Cruise was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.200 --> 00:11:50.400
a massive success and he was there early, but he&nbsp;
wanted to pursue something bigger. “We are running&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.400 --> 00:11:54.880
out of resources on the planet” He clearly cares&nbsp;
deeply about space and what it can unlock for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.880 --> 00:11:59.200
humanity broadly. Lots of people aspire to work in&nbsp;
amazing industries and have an impact when they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.200 --> 00:12:04.480
young, but then they get caught on a particular&nbsp;
treadmill and lose sight of their original goal.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.480 --> 00:12:08.640
Sorhab took a lot of risk by switching industries&nbsp;
after leaving Cruise. He probably could have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.640 --> 00:12:12.960
worked at any self-driving company, but he&nbsp;
chose to pursue something bigger. And he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.960 --> 00:12:17.840
didn’t just risk a few resume bullet points.&nbsp;
He actually took out a second mortgage just to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.840 --> 00:12:22.880
pay for SpaceRyde’s rocket testing facility.&nbsp;
No one would rent him land to test rockets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.880 --> 00:12:26.320
which is honestly kind of understandable, so he&nbsp;
just did it himself. I love that attitude. When&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.320 --> 00:12:30.880
you’re working at a big company, enjoying free&nbsp;
lunches, it can be hard to take a leap of faith&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.880 --> 00:12:34.960
and go build something in a garage. But I’m&nbsp;
certain that when Sohrab looks back on his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.960 --> 00:12:49.120
time building rockets in his garage, he won’t&nbsp;
regret one minute of it. Thanks for watching.

